.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt CACHESTAT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cachestat
.Nd count the pages of a file in memory
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/syscall.h
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fo syscall
.Fa SYS_cachestat
.Fa "unsigned int fd"
.Fa "struct cachestat_range *range"
.Fa "struct cachestat *cstat"
.Fa "unsigned int flags"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn cachestat
counts the pages of the file
.Fa fd
is open on that are in memory, as Linux's call does, and stores the
counts in
.Fa cstat .
Both records are those of Linux, of 64-bit fields alone, and have the
same layout for an i386 program:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct cachestat_range {
	uint64_t off;	/* first byte */
	uint64_t len;	/* bytes; 0 reaches to the end */
};

struct cachestat {
	uint64_t nr_cache;
	uint64_t nr_dirty;
	uint64_t nr_writeback;
	uint64_t nr_evicted;
	uint64_t nr_recently_evicted;
};
.Ed
.Pp
The range covers the pages from the one holding byte
.Fa off
to the one holding its last byte, and stops at the end of the file.
.Va nr_cache
counts those in memory and
.Va nr_dirty
those of them not yet written back.
A tmpfs or memfd page is in memory once it is allocated, and is dirty;
a page of
.Xr memfd_secret 2
is in memory but never dirty, as on Linux.
An ext2 page is in memory when the buffer cache holds every block of
it, and dirty when one of them waits to be written.
Rach2 keeps no record of pages it has dropped, and writes a block back
without a state between dirty and clean, so
.Va nr_writeback ,
.Va nr_evicted
and
.Va nr_recently_evicted
are always 0.
What is not a file of a mounted file system, a pipe or a socket among
them, counts no page.
.Pp
The caller must have
.Fa fd
open for writing, own the file, be privileged, or be allowed to write
the file by its mode, as Linux 6.12's stable kernels require.
.Fa flags
must be 0.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
0 on success.
On failure -1 is returned with
.Va errno
set; the kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh ERRORS
The checks run in this order, as on Linux.
.Bl -tag -width "[EFAULT]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not open, or is open with
.Dv O_PATH .
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa range
cannot be read, or
.Fa cstat
cannot be written.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller may not count the file's pages, as above.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa flags
is not 0.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr madvise 2 ,
.Xr mincore 2
.Sh STANDARDS
Linux 6.5 added the call.
